@@ -191,7 +191,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "Sub-fascia is the structural framing board, usually a piece of dimensional lumber, that runs behind the visible fascia trim and caps the ends of the rafters or trusses. It is the load-bearing piece; the fascia board homeowners see is a decorative and functional cover nailed over it. Sub-fascia gives the roof edge its shape and gives the fascia and gutters something solid to attach to. Because it sits hidden behind the trim, damage to it usually surfaces only once the fascia or roofing is opened up for repair.",
     body:
-      "Every rafter tail at the edge of a roof needs something to tie it together and give the roofline a straight, finished edge. That job belongs to the sub-fascia, a run of solid lumber nailed across the ends of the rafters or trusses before any trim goes on.\n\nThe fascia board most homeowners recognize, the flat trim strip that gutters mount to, is fastened directly over the sub-fascia. The fascia is a cosmetic and protective layer; the sub-fascia underneath is the actual structure holding that edge of the roof together.\n\nBecause the sub-fascia sits behind the fascia and often behind the soffit as well, it stays completely hidden in normal conditions. Nobody sees it from the ground, and it is not something a roofer checks on a routine visual look from the yard.\n\nThe trouble is that water finds its way to the sub-fascia the same way it finds every hidden edge on a roof. A clogged gutter that overflows, a cracked fascia board, or a failed drip edge can all funnel moisture back against the sub-fascia for months before anyone notices.\n\nOnce wet, the sub-fascia rots the same as any other lumber, and because it carries real structural load, rot there is more serious than a cosmetic fascia problem. A soft or crumbling sub-fascia often means the rafter tails themselves are compromised.\n\nSub-fascia damage is usually found the hard way, during a fascia or gutter repair when the wood behind the trim turns out to be soft. Replacing it means rebuilding part of the roof's edge structure rather than just swapping trim, so ask any contractor who finds it to document the damage with photos before the repair covers it back up.",
