@@ -209,7 +209,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "Three-tab shingles are a flat, single-layer asphalt shingle cut with two slots so each strip appears to be three separate rectangular tabs once installed. They are lighter and less expensive than architectural shingles but generally carry a shorter warranty and hold up less well to wind and impact. Once the standard roofing product, three-tab shingles have been largely replaced by architectural shingles on new construction and most replacements, though they still appear on some existing roofs and budget projects.",
     body:
-      "A three-tab shingle is a single flat layer of asphalt, cut along its lower edge into three even tabs so it lays as a uniform grid pattern once the whole roof is shingled.\n\nThat flat, single-layer construction is what separates it from an architectural shingle, which laminates two or more layers together for a thicker, shadowed look. A three-tab roof reads as flat and uniform from the street rather than dimensional.\n\nBeing lighter and simpler to manufacture, three-tab shingles have always sat at the lower end of the price range, which made them the standard choice for decades on both new construction and replacements.\n\nThe tradeoff for that lower cost is durability. A single asphalt layer flexes and wears faster than a laminated shingle, and three-tab products usually carry shorter manufacturer warranties, often in the 20 to 25 year range, with less resistance to high wind. They also tend to show granule loss and edge wear sooner, since there is only one layer of protection instead of two.\n\nMost manufacturers have shifted their product lines toward the laminated, dimensional style described above as the mainstream option, and many roofers now install three-tab only on a specific budget request or to match an existing three-tab roof during a repair.\n\nFor a homeowner, seeing three-tab shingles quoted today signals the lowest tier of a bid, and it is fair to ask what a step up in shingle style would add to the price before deciding.\n\nIf a repair is meant to match an existing three-tab roof rather than replace it, confirming the manufacturer and profile keeps the patched section blending in with the rest of the slope.",
