@@ -236,7 +236,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "A gable roof is the classic triangle-shaped roof style, with two sloped sides meeting at a central ridge and a flat, vertical triangular wall, the gable, at each end. It is simpler and generally less expensive to build and shingle than a hip roof because it has fewer seams and hips. The tradeoff sits at the gable ends themselves, which can be more vulnerable to wind if not properly braced, particularly in exposed locations.",
     body:
-      "A gable roof is the shape most people picture first when they think of a roof: two flat slopes rising to meet at a single ridge line, with a triangular wall, the gable, filling in each end.\n\nThat simple two-plane shape is what makes a gable roof straightforward to frame and to shingle. There are fewer transitions, fewer hips, and generally less total roof surface than a hip roof over the same size home, which keeps both material and labor costs lower. Fewer seams also means fewer hand-flashed details, which speeds up both a new installation and a future re-roof.\n\nThe gable ends themselves are a distinct part of the structure, not roof surface at all, but a flat vertical wall that often carries a gable vent for attic ventilation.\n\nBecause that wall stands straight up rather than sloping away, it presents a flatter target to wind coming from the side, which is why gable ends need proper bracing and, in exposed areas, extra fastening at the roof-to-wall connection.\n\nA well-built gable roof handles hard storms fine when the framing and flashing at the gable end are done right. Problems tend to show up when that bracing was skipped or when flashing where the roof meets the gable wall was installed poorly.\n\nFor a homeowner, a gable roof's simpler shape is generally a cost advantage on a replacement quote, and confirming that the gable ends are properly vented and braced is the one detail that affects both comfort and storm resilience.\n\nA gable end with a working vent and solid bracing costs little extra to build right the first time, and a great deal more to fix after a storm finds the weak spot.",
