@@ -254,7 +254,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "The eave is the lower edge of a roof that overhangs the exterior wall, running along the horizontal side of the house rather than the sloped gable ends. It is where drip edge, gutters, and the soffit all come together to control water as it leaves the roof. Because rain concentrates and drains off at the eave, it is one of the roof's highest-wear areas. Codes in colder regions require an ice barrier there against ice dams, and in warmer climates quality installations add ice and water shield at the eave by choice.",
     body:
-      "The eave is the bottom edge of a sloped roof, the horizontal line where the roof overhangs the wall along the long sides of the house. It is distinct from the rake edge, which runs up the sloped, gable side of the roof instead.\n\nSeveral separate systems converge at the eave. The soffit closes off the underside of the overhang, the fascia caps the rafter ends and holds the gutters, and drip edge guides water off the shingles and into those gutters rather than back under them.\n\nBecause every drop of water on a sloped roof eventually runs down to an eave, that edge handles far more moving water over its length than the open field of the roof above it.\n\nThat concentrated flow is why the eave gets extra protection. Building codes in colder regions require an ice barrier at the eave to guard against ice dams, and in warmer climates without that code trigger, quality installations still add a course of ice and water shield there by choice, on top of standard drip edge and underlayment.\n\nIt is also why gutters are hung at the eave rather than anywhere else. A gutter's whole job is to catch the water shedding off the eave before it reaches the ground and the foundation.\n\nFor a homeowner, the eave is the place to check after a hard rain, since so many separate systems share those few inches of roofline.\n\nWater pouring over the gutter, staining on the fascia, or a soft spot in the soffit there usually points to a problem right at the eave rather than somewhere higher on the roof.",
