@@ -308,7 +308,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "A gutter apron is a style of drip edge shaped specifically for the eave, with a longer, more sharply bent lower flange designed to extend past the fascia and reach down into the back of the gutter trough. Unlike standard drip edge, which simply directs water off the roof edge, a gutter apron aims that water directly into the gutter, closing the gap where water can otherwise splash or run behind the gutter's back wall. It is installed at the eave only, not along the rake.",
     body:
-      "Standard drip edge does its job at any roof edge, giving water a clean break off the shingles instead of letting it curl back underneath. At the eave, where gutters are mounted, a gutter apron takes that same idea a step further.\n\nA gutter apron is bent with a longer lower flange than typical drip edge, angled to extend past the fascia board and drop down inside the back lip of the gutter itself, rather than stopping at the edge of the roof.\n\nThat extra reach matters because a standard drip edge can leave a small gap between the roof edge and the back of the gutter, a gap where water can jump the gutter entirely during a heavy downpour or splash behind it over time.\n\nBy reaching into the gutter trough, a gutter apron closes that gap and makes sure the water the drip edge redirects actually lands inside the gutter instead of behind it, where it would run down the fascia and toward the wall.\n\nNot every re-roof specifies a gutter apron by name: some crews substitute a slightly oversized standard drip edge instead, which narrows the gap but does not fully close it the way a true gutter apron does.\n\nFor a homeowner, a gutter apron is one of those small upgrades that shows up in the fine print of a quality re-roof.\n\nIf gutters keep showing water stains on the fascia behind them even though they are clean, check for a missing or undersized gutter apron before assuming the gutters themselves are at fault.",
