@@ -317,7 +317,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "A soffit vent is an intake vent built into the underside of the roof overhang, letting outside air flow up into the attic. Paired with an exhaust vent higher on the roof, such as a ridge vent, soffit vents are the intake half of a balanced attic ventilation system. They come as small round or rectangular vents cut into individual soffit panels, or as a continuous perforated strip running the full length of the soffit, and either style is only useful if the airflow above it is not blocked.",
     body:
-      "Attic ventilation works as a two-part system, drawing cooler outside air in low on the roof and letting hot, humid air escape higher up. Soffit vents are the intake side of that system, built into the underside of the roof overhang where fresh air can be pulled in.\n\nThey show up in two common forms: individual round or rectangular vents punched into separate soffit panels, spaced evenly along the eave, or a continuous perforated soffit panel that vents along its entire length instead of at intervals.\n\nEither style depends on a clear path for that air once it gets past the soffit. Insulation piled against the roof deck at the eave, or baffles that are missing or crushed, can block the airflow before it ever reaches the open attic.\n\nThat intake air is what makes an exhaust vent, like a ridge vent, actually work. A ridge vent with no functioning soffit intake has nothing pulling fresh air behind it, and the attic stays stagnant no matter how good the exhaust vent is.\n\nSoffit vents also need to stay clear from the outside. Paint clogging the perforations, insulation stuffed against them from inside, or debris packed into individual vents all choke off the intake even when the vent itself looks intact.\n\nFor a homeowner, checking that soffit vents are actually open, both from the outside and from inside the attic, is a simple way to confirm the ventilation system is doing its job.\n\nA vent that looks fine from the driveway can still be blocked from behind by insulation, which is why the inside check matters as much as the outside one.",
