@@ -443,7 +443,7 @@ export const GLOSSARY_TERMS: GlossaryTerm[] = [
     shortAnswer:
       "Algae-resistant shingles are asphalt shingles manufactured with copper or other metal-infused granules that discourage the growth of algae, the organism responsible for the dark streaks and stains that develop on many roofs over time. The metal granules release trace amounts of a compound that is inhospitable to algae as rain washes across the shingle, slowing new growth without eliminating existing stains. They are especially useful in humid, shaded climates where algae streaking is common, and most major manufacturers back the algae-resistant coating with its own limited warranty.",
     body:
-      "Those dark streaks that gradually appear on many asphalt roofs, often mistaken for dirt or soot, are usually a form of algae, an organism that thrives in warm, humid conditions and spreads across a shingle surface over years.\n\nAlgae-resistant shingles are built to slow that growth from the start. Manufacturers blend copper or other metal particles into a portion of the shingle's granules, and as rain washes across the roof, those particles release a small amount of a compound that discourages new algae from taking hold.\n\nThe effect is preventive rather than a fix for stains already present. Algae-resistant granules slow future growth; they do not reverse or remove streaking that has already developed on an older, non-resistant roof.\n\nHumid, shaded climates with plenty of rainfall are exactly the conditions algae favors, which makes algae-resistant shingles a practical upgrade for homes with significant tree cover or a north-facing slope that stays damp longer after a rain. A roof with heavy tree cover on one side and full sun on the other can show a visible difference in streaking between the two slopes over time.\n\nBecause the resistant compound wears down gradually with each rainfall over the years, most manufacturers back algae resistance with its own separate, often shorter, limited warranty rather than folding it into the main shingle warranty.\n\nFor a homeowner choosing shingles for a new roof, algae resistance is a natural fit for a shaded lot or a roof with a history of streaking.\n\nThe upgrade is a modest cost difference against the total roof price, addressing one of the most visible, most common complaints homeowners have about an aging roof.",
+      "Those dark streaks that gradually appear on many asphalt roofs, often mistaken for dirt or soot, are usually a form of algae, an organism that thrives in warm, humid conditions and spreads across a shingle surface over years.\n\nAlgae-resistant shingles are built to slow that growth from the start. Manufacturers blend copper or other metal particles into a portion of the shingle's granules, and as rain washes across the roof, those particles release a small amount of a compound that discourages new algae from taking hold.\n\nThe effect is preventive rather than a fix for stains already present. Algae-resistant granules slow future growth; they do not reverse or remove streaking that has already developed on an older, non-resistant roof.\n\nHumid, shaded climates with plenty of rainfall are exactly the conditions algae favors, which makes algae-resistant shingles a practical upgrade for homes with significant tree cover or a north-facing slope that stays damp longer after a rain. A roof with heavy tree cover on one side and full sun on the other can show a visible difference in streaking between the two slopes over time.\n\nBecause the resistant compound wears down gradually with each rainfall over the years, most manufacturers back algae resistance with its own separate, often shorter, limited warranty rather than folding it into the main shingle warranty.\n\nFor a homeowner choosing shingles for a [new roof](/services/roof-replacement), algae resistance is a natural fit for a shaded lot or a roof with a history of streaking.\n\nThe upgrade is a modest cost difference against the total roof price, addressing one of the most visible, most common complaints homeowners have about an aging roof.",
